WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.971
-Welcome back, everybody,
to "Corrections,"

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:10.643
and let's get started.
We, last week in "Corrections"

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:15.615
I referenced
an O. Henry story.

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.483
I did not realize,
O. Henry, of course,

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.886
known for writing short stories

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:24.190
that had twist endings,
if you will.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:29.695
I did not realize that,
O. Henry was a pen name.

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:34.533
Nom de plume,
as Shoemaker likes to say.

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:37.237
His real name, Henry.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:43.276
And he got his pen name

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:46.512
because he would give
his stories to his wife.

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:50.150
And when she would get
to the twist endings.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.987
She would say, "Oh! Henry!"

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.994
A couple of people,
a lot of people

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:03.429
caught the reference
to "The Gift of the Magi.

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:07.600
Two people said, "Hey, you stole
that from the Muppets'

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.170
1978 Christmas Special."

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:19.145
Well, this is no. No judgment.
This is a true story.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.114
And I'm going to stop there
a couple of weeks ago,

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.183
I said this is a true story
that happened

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:25.117
and a couple of people said,

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:26.953
once you say
this is a true story,

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.721
you don't have to say
"that happened"

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:30.456
because that's redundant

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:33.259
and people who watch
"Corrections"

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.662
want to keep it moving.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.065
This is a true story --

00:01:40.065 --> 00:01:44.337
My dad, every Christmas
would tell my brother and I

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:49.875
a story about Yussi the Yak and
Morris the Moose

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:51.778
who were best friends.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.880
And Morris had
beautiful antlers,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.648
and what he wanted more
than anything was a bow

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:58.484
for his antlers.
And Yussi was a yak

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:01.754
who wanted a brush
for his beautiful yak hair.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.589
And, I think you know
where this is going,

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:07.393
Morris sold his antlers
to get the brush,

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.496
and Yussi stole his hair

00:02:09.496 --> 00:02:12.465
to get the bow
for Morris's antlers.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.333
One of the reasons
they said a red flag

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:16.135
that this is maybe not
a real story

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:17.704
is that it was
a Christmas story,

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:20.340
and the characters names
were Yussi and Morris.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:26.746
But the first time --
I can't remember what age --

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:29.716
the first time we were
assigned "Gift of a Magi,"

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:37.356
I read it and my reaction was,
"He stole this from my dad."

00:02:37.356 --> 00:02:39.458
and I went up to my teacher
and I had to explain it to her,

00:02:39.458 --> 00:02:42.061
and she crestfallen.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.130
Because obviously,
this is an author she respected

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:48.333
and book she'd assigned over the
years, and I'll never forget.

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:53.539
What she said when it dawned on
her that he was a thief.

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:57.076
She said, "Oh. Henry."

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.216
I talked about how Shoemaker's
never on camera,

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:06.652
one person said, "Wait, wasn't
Shoemaker on camera

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:09.221
when you showed the picture
of him in a tank top?"

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:13.426
Oop, you've messed up
your Mikes.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:16.362
We have a Mike Scallions
and a Mike Shoemaker.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.063
They share a name

00:03:18.063 --> 00:03:20.833
and they're unpleasant
in very different ways.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:25.705
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.840
When Scallions says,
"Sun's out, guns out,"

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.811
its tank top,
when Shoemaker says it,

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.813
it's like a snub-nosed revolver.

00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:37.516
We talked about Pavarotti.

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:40.620
I called him a B-list celebrity,
people were really upset.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.989
I clarified that
when a person is Italian

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:46.125
the B means bellissimo.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.860
I was also told
you could call him --

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:51.897
It would be very insulting
to call him a C-list celebrity.

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:54.267
But you could,
in opera terms,

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:59.304
you could call him
a high-C-list celebrity.

00:03:59.304 --> 00:04:02.308
As that is the note
he was very famous for hitting.

00:04:04.543 --> 00:04:07.313
Bird's nostrils
are called nares.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.654
That comes up, obviously,
because.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:19.826
We keep showing
this photo of the, um,

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:21.527
pigeon.
Can we show the pigeon here?

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:22.962
There we go.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:27.633
Those are, those are its nares
right there underneath.

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:31.036
Those are the exposed nares.

00:04:31.036 --> 00:04:33.840
And what we were told it was
what was really troubling

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.911
was that the bricks were in
the wrong going the wrong way.

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:39.945
Some people reached out
and said,

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:41.380
"That's actually
a style of brick."

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.450
It's called soldier style
when you do bricks that way.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:48.787
And then someone else said,
"Bud, I hate to run to

00:04:48.787 --> 00:04:51.491
the defense
of your graphics department,

00:04:51.491 --> 00:04:56.028
but fully, fully exists
in New York City."

00:04:56.028 --> 00:05:00.266
So. The only thing I'll say
is just because graphics

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:04.036
was right on this doesn't mean
they're good people.

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:07.006
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.674
Because you show the pigeon
one time.

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:12.044
This is truly what happened.
So let's the pigeon.

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:13.312
And I saw it
and I was frustrated

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:14.981
because people were
criticizing the bricks,

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:16.215
whether right or wrong.

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.816
Our viewers criticized
the bricks, and I said,

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:19.652
"Hey, can you just can you
put it on its side?"

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.420
And this is what
they sent back.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:29.495
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.664
We also -- this is
a few weeks back now.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:35.668
We wrote a thing saying
it's like Pete Rose

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:38.738
being on second base,
calling his bookie.

00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:41.040
And...

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.576
it was a photo of Pete Rose
who didn't play second base,

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:47.913
standing near second base
with a first baseman's glove,

00:05:47.913 --> 00:05:50.249
and a lot of people said,
"Hey, what are you doing?

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:53.218
Pete Rose didn't play second and
that's a first baseman's glove."

00:05:53.218 --> 00:05:57.023
And the reality is we,

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.258
when we wrote it,
we meant that he had doubled

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.093
and was standing
on second base

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:04.130
because there's two ways
to do it in baseball.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:10.102
But unless you are to the word
with the graphics department,

00:06:10.102 --> 00:06:11.571
they will [Bleep] you.

00:06:11.571 --> 00:06:13.906
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:17.109
And I went down because,
again, I get angry,

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:21.381
I go down, knock, knock, knock,
and they're like, "Come in."

00:06:23.782 --> 00:06:26.452
And again, I --
Shame on me for teeing it up,

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:27.987
and I'm like, "Do you guys
not know the difference

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:29.488
between first base
and second base?"

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:33.426
And they're like,
"Oh, no you want to show us?"

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:38.397
And then thankfully we have
we have an HR person

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:40.299
that we've basically
just put in the booth,

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:42.468
the graphics booth,
because like 95 percent

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:45.470
of our complaints
come out of there.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:48.274
So there's just this guy, Gene,
who just reads

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:49.608
and he's like reading
the daily news

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:51.878
and he's always like,
Hey, cut it out.

00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:00.452
And I was burning graphics
on the fact

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:02.855
that we have
a satellite that exploded

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:04.290
and then the pieces fell down,

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.092
and in my criticism
then I revealed

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:09.194
I don't know anything
about outer space,

00:07:09.194 --> 00:07:10.796
and I said the pieces
wouldn't fall down

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:11.930
because there's
no gravity out there.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.033
There is gravity in space

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.935
and the pieces
just wouldn't fall straight down

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:21.006
because of orbital speed
and lateral velocity.

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:23.842
And then someone else
mentioned inertia,

00:07:23.842 --> 00:07:25.444
and I thought, that's
a good thing to talk about.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:29.681
Because, I kind of feel
like at its core, graphics,

00:07:29.681 --> 00:07:30.883
I'm sorry, not graphics.

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:35.053
"Corrections" is is sort
of a form of inertia.

00:07:35.053 --> 00:07:39.258
In that we're not actually
getting anywhere.

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:41.827
Like this is the act
of getting better,

00:07:41.827 --> 00:07:44.964
but I don't, I fear that there's
not any actual --

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:48.501
For example, the Irish
prime minister

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:51.303
came back up again
because you guys,

00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:55.373
he was here and I then,
even after having worked

00:07:55.373 --> 00:07:59.278
so hard on the pronunciation,
I got it wrong again.

00:07:59.278 --> 00:08:01.547 align:end size:78% position:78%
It's Taoiseach.
[Tee shock]

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:04.484
But I even tried last week
to watch, and I made the mistake

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:06.552
because you --
a lot of Irish viewers

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.321
have made it very clear
it's Taoiseach

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:10.389
and then I made a mistake
like watching.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:12.458
You can try to watch YouTube
videos where people say it,

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:16.329
but they're all like really old
Irish men

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.664
who are very the way
they tell you, it's like,

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.066
[ Irish accent ] It's very easy
to say the word

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:23.969
just push the bottom
of your tongue

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:26.305
against the outside
of your teeth

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:29.641
and press the air
out through your nose,

00:08:29.641 --> 00:08:32.845
in your mouth in equal measure.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:36.148
But don't say
the top part of the T.

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:39.251
Don't say the part
that goes across.

00:08:39.251 --> 00:08:40.386
Just do this part.

00:08:40.386 --> 00:08:44.457
Just think of
a soldier-style brick.

00:08:46.658 --> 00:08:48.928
[ Normal voice ]
Also, someone said, you know,
you don't have to say Taoiseach,

00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:50.563
you could just say his name.

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.534
Here's the thing,
that's his name.

00:08:57.203 --> 00:09:00.406
I think it's a trap
that look super easy,

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:03.809
but something's
going on right there.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.644
There it is.

00:09:05.644 --> 00:09:09.015
And I feel like
if I said this the way

00:09:09.015 --> 00:09:13.052
I think it is, you'd be like,
it's "Me shock."

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:19.624
He was here and nobody got me.

00:09:19.624 --> 00:09:23.495
They'll be like.
"Oh, were you not?

00:09:23.495 --> 00:09:25.731
They were doing a table read
of Wedding Crashers 2,

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:29.869
and neither Owen
or Vince were there.

00:09:29.869 --> 00:09:31.504
We didn't think to get you."

00:09:36.675 --> 00:09:38.811
This is my favorite comment
of the week,

00:09:38.811 --> 00:09:42.648
because someone's frustrated.
Just wrote this.

00:09:47.986 --> 00:09:49.622
I don't, I don't know.

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:58.530
Tried to call Samberg last week.
He didn't answer.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:01.734
Pretty upset.

00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:04.536
Because when I went to NBC
with "Corrections"

00:10:04.536 --> 00:10:08.273
and they gave us
the seed money for it.

00:10:08.273 --> 00:10:10.509
The promise was that
I would be able to call

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:13.578
my celebrity friends
and get them to answer.

00:10:13.578 --> 00:10:18.551
And, well, you know, what's he
got going on that he can't

00:10:18.551 --> 00:10:21.720
answer a phone call from me?

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.323
You write down words
that rhyme with nutsack.

00:10:24.323 --> 00:10:28.861
[ Laughter ]

00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:32.698
So I almost I don't know if I
want to give him another chance.

00:10:32.698 --> 00:10:37.970
But at the same time.
You know, I love him.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:39.605
He owes me.

00:10:40.639 --> 00:10:50.449
[ Cell phone rings ]

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.289
Nope.

00:10:57.289 --> 00:10:58.658
That's how it goes, guys.

00:11:01.994 --> 00:11:04.196
It's Bachman-Turner Overdrive.

00:11:04.196 --> 00:11:07.099
[ Cell phone dings ]

00:11:07.099 --> 00:11:08.501
Not him.

00:11:08.501 --> 00:11:11.370
[ Laughter ]

00:11:18.477 --> 00:11:21.647
It's Bachman-Turner Overdrive.

00:11:21.647 --> 00:11:24.049
So I'm glad we have taken care
of that business.

00:11:24.049 --> 00:11:26.618
[ Light laughter ]

00:11:26.618 --> 00:11:29.755
Also, the Mets second place --
second baseman

00:11:29.755 --> 00:11:31.390
was Wally Backman.

00:11:33.759 --> 00:11:36.461
And I'd show a picture of him,
but...

00:11:36.461 --> 00:11:39.198
Graphics would probably
put them in a [Bleep]

00:11:39.198 --> 00:11:41.100
catcher's mitt
and a football helmet.

00:11:44.369 --> 00:11:47.306
We did, um,
a surprise inspection,

00:11:47.306 --> 00:11:49.942
where we read jokes
that our writers had written

00:11:49.942 --> 00:11:52.978
that were too, did not meet
the standard of the show

00:11:52.978 --> 00:11:56.281
and we wanted to sort of
shame them by reading them.

00:11:56.281 --> 00:11:57.916
A lot of people wrote,
"This isn't a correction,

00:11:57.916 --> 00:12:01.019
but we do feel like Ally Hord's
joke was really good

00:12:01.019 --> 00:12:03.688
and you shouldn't
have judged it poorly."

00:12:03.688 --> 00:12:05.357
Her joke was,
"According to a new study,

00:12:05.357 --> 00:12:07.526
mice have feelings of sadness
and depression.

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:10.162
Asks scientists, 'Did
they say if it was because

00:12:10.162 --> 00:12:13.398
of the cancer we gave them?'"

00:12:13.398 --> 00:12:15.034
People really liked that joke.

00:12:17.035 --> 00:12:19.571
Ian wrote, "In honor of
Disney World's 50th anniversary,

00:12:19.571 --> 00:12:22.541
Southwest Airlines has unveiled
a commemorative custom

00:12:22.541 --> 00:12:24.176
designed plane that features
an outline

00:12:24.176 --> 00:12:26.412
of Cinderella Castle
on the outside,

00:12:26.412 --> 00:12:30.416
but heads up the inside
is still full pumpkin."

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:33.552
Not a word of support
for that joke.

00:12:33.552 --> 00:12:36.888
[ Laughter ]

00:12:36.888 --> 00:12:40.092
I aired, and I was going
to try to burn Ian for it.

00:12:40.092 --> 00:12:42.361
I made a mistake
when I said it on the show

00:12:42.361 --> 00:12:43.696
and I went back and looked.

00:12:43.696 --> 00:12:47.366
I said, Cinderella's Castle
and a lot of people.

00:12:47.366 --> 00:12:50.435
One guy said to me,
It's not Cinderella's Castle,

00:12:50.435 --> 00:12:55.908
it's Cinderella Castle.
It is not her castle.

00:12:55.908 --> 00:12:58.710
It is Prince Charming's castle
named after her,

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:01.814
but she just lives there.
It's not Cinderella's Castle.

00:13:01.814 --> 00:13:04.917
She just lives there
at Prince Charming's castle.

00:13:04.917 --> 00:13:07.086
And there's one thing
I will say about the guy

00:13:07.086 --> 00:13:10.022
who wrote me that,
he's not married.

00:13:11.223 --> 00:13:12.524
[ Laughter ]

00:13:12.524 --> 00:13:15.528
Because, you know,
I think at some point

00:13:15.528 --> 00:13:17.463
it was Cinderella's Castle.

00:13:17.463 --> 00:13:19.464
And maybe when she first
moved in, he was like,

00:13:19.464 --> 00:13:21.400
So I'm going to name it
after you, Cinderella's Castle.

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:24.703
But, like, no, it's okay.
You know, it's like mine.

00:13:24.703 --> 00:13:28.140
You can just live here
and we'll call it

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.142
Cinderella Castle,
but I just want to make sure.

00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:31.977
And then like a week later,
one of his friends are like,

00:13:31.977 --> 00:13:34.380
Did I see an apostrophe
S on the gate?

00:13:35.414 --> 00:13:40.218
[ Light laughter ]

00:13:40.218 --> 00:13:43.121
My Randy Newman now people
have moved on from Bill Cosby

00:13:43.121 --> 00:13:46.825
and said he sounds
like a Jamaican.

00:13:46.825 --> 00:13:48.794
[ As Newman ] Get up, stand up.

00:13:48.794 --> 00:13:51.997
[ Laughter ]

00:13:51.997 --> 00:13:59.371
[ Normal voice ] I'd like to do
something a little...

00:13:59.371 --> 00:14:01.607
I'd like to do something
a little different.

00:14:04.643 --> 00:14:08.780
Can we put up a title card,
maybe in a beautiful script?

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.582
That says, "Reflections."

00:14:10.582 --> 00:14:12.550
And when we put it up,
can we play some

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:14.219
sort of thoughtful music?

00:14:14.219 --> 00:14:20.526
[ Fanfare plays ]

00:14:22.361 --> 00:14:26.531
Welcome to "Reflections."
We've talked about this.

00:14:26.531 --> 00:14:29.468
Monday will be our first show
back with audiences.

00:14:29.468 --> 00:14:34.740
We have done 241 shows
without an audience

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:41.613
after we left on March 12, 2020.

00:14:41.613 --> 00:14:46.218
And I have a fair amount
of nervousness

00:14:46.218 --> 00:14:48.821
and trepidation
about audiences coming back.

00:14:48.821 --> 00:14:49.988
I just want to be honest
about that.

00:14:49.988 --> 00:14:53.258
I will say that most of of you

00:14:53.258 --> 00:14:56.895
who engage
with "Corrections" seem,

00:14:56.895 --> 00:14:59.498
to not want the audience back,

00:14:59.498 --> 00:15:01.467
which is not super
surprising me

00:15:01.467 --> 00:15:07.305
because it always struck me,
that based on the way you are,

00:15:07.305 --> 00:15:09.341
you don't like people.

00:15:12.644 --> 00:15:14.746
I want to assure you,
the "Corrections" will always be

00:15:14.746 --> 00:15:16.915
without an audience.

00:15:16.915 --> 00:15:19.784
And...

00:15:19.784 --> 00:15:21.453
having an audience back...

00:15:23.421 --> 00:15:27.493
is very exciting in the way
that the last --

00:15:27.493 --> 00:15:29.495
I know, it's strange to say
these last 18 months

00:15:29.495 --> 00:15:33.832
has been really exciting
for terrible reasons,

00:15:33.832 --> 00:15:35.467
but we've had all these
different challenges

00:15:35.467 --> 00:15:36.768
that we've had to overcome.

00:15:36.768 --> 00:15:37.936
And I think one of the biggest
fears when you do a show

00:15:37.936 --> 00:15:39.371
like this is that
they're going to be boring.

00:15:39.371 --> 00:15:41.239
And nothing about the last
two years have been boring.

00:15:41.239 --> 00:15:43.776
I mean, we did it from an attic

00:15:43.776 --> 00:15:47.278
and we did it
from my in-law's house

00:15:47.278 --> 00:15:49.214
and I was worried
last September.

00:15:49.214 --> 00:15:50.482
I was deeply worried
last September

00:15:50.482 --> 00:15:52.717
about how the show
would feel coming back

00:15:52.717 --> 00:15:54.219
in the studio
without an audience.

00:15:54.219 --> 00:15:57.956
And I think it's been
pretty wonderful

00:15:57.956 --> 00:16:01.426
and it's been really cool
to have you here

00:16:01.426 --> 00:16:04.329
with us in this intimate space.
And I think, you know,

00:16:04.329 --> 00:16:08.299
we all know
what Buck's laugh sounds like,

00:16:08.299 --> 00:16:14.205
and we all know what
Wally's laugh sounds like.

00:16:14.205 --> 00:16:23.414
Still waiting on Paul.

00:16:23.414 --> 00:16:29.621
Everybody, but.

00:16:29.621 --> 00:16:33.925
I should also stress, you know,
I made some jokes about.

00:16:33.925 --> 00:16:37.663
Wally being on the show last.

00:16:37.663 --> 00:16:39.064
It turns out we did
not look closely

00:16:39.064 --> 00:16:40.666
at the last contract
we signed with Wally,

00:16:40.666 --> 00:16:42.835
and there's some
pretty onerous language.

00:16:42.835 --> 00:16:46.238
And if he is not on camera,
a fair amount.

00:16:46.238 --> 00:16:49.274
He has threatened that
he'll start using white Sharpie.

00:16:49.274 --> 00:16:52.277
[ Laughter ]

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:01.352
So the goal,

00:17:01.352 --> 00:17:04.456
is to try to take everything
that we've loved

00:17:04.456 --> 00:17:09.594
about the last almost two years
of doing this show

00:17:09.594 --> 00:17:12.964
outside the way we were
so used to doing it,

00:17:12.964 --> 00:17:15.267
for the first six years
of doing this show,

00:17:15.267 --> 00:17:16.868
so take that and bring that back

00:17:16.868 --> 00:17:18.303
with the excitement
of having a live audience.

00:17:18.303 --> 00:17:21.373
That's the challenge
and I think, you know.

00:17:21.373 --> 00:17:25.810
you know, there is a expression.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:30.249
You know. Necessity has a name,
and it is inventor-mom.

00:17:32.584 --> 00:17:37.955
And so much of what
has been fun about

00:17:37.955 --> 00:17:40.358
this era of the show is things
you have to figure out

00:17:40.358 --> 00:17:43.961
because of the hurdles that have
been put in front of us

00:17:43.961 --> 00:17:46.498
by circumstance and...

00:17:48.466 --> 00:17:50.068
I'm looking forward
to the next part of it.

00:17:50.068 --> 00:17:53.972
And I hope you'll
come along with us.

00:17:53.972 --> 00:17:58.443
The weirdest thing was,
I never, swear to God,

00:17:58.443 --> 00:18:01.746
never read a YouTube comment
about our show

00:18:01.746 --> 00:18:04.949
until I started doing it in the
attic, and then I realize now

00:18:04.949 --> 00:18:07.352
it's very easy to track.
[ Cell phone dings ]

00:18:07.352 --> 00:18:08.554
Nope.

00:18:08.554 --> 00:18:11.890
[ Laughter ]

00:18:15.727 --> 00:18:19.631
I started reading them
because I was desperate

00:18:19.631 --> 00:18:21.266
for some level of feedback,
right,

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:22.667
you do the show
in front of an audience.

00:18:22.667 --> 00:18:24.703
You don't walk off every night
wondering how it went.

00:18:24.703 --> 00:18:26.037
You kind of in your head,
know how it went,

00:18:26.037 --> 00:18:27.639
but you start doing it
in attic and you think,

00:18:27.639 --> 00:18:30.575
Oh, I should try to check in
and see how this is going.

00:18:30.575 --> 00:18:35.713
And so I waded into what
everyone said was a cesspool,

00:18:35.713 --> 00:18:39.150
and I found some very nice
people there.

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:40.419
And I want to.

00:18:45.324 --> 00:18:49.194
[ Laughter ]

00:18:51.396 --> 00:18:53.432
Thank you so much for the way

00:18:53.432 --> 00:18:55.066
that you've engaged
with this show.

00:18:55.066 --> 00:18:57.903
Because we do feel like it's
it's better and it's different,

00:18:57.903 --> 00:18:59.637
and we've enjoyed
being on that journey.

00:18:59.637 --> 00:19:01.873
And that's the end
of "Reflections"

00:19:01.873 --> 00:19:03.875
and if we could just again
do the script

00:19:03.875 --> 00:19:08.079
and maybe something that matches
the emotion of the moment.

00:19:08.079 --> 00:19:14.386
[ Fanfare plays ]

00:19:14.786 --> 00:19:17.488
Lastly, I'd like to close.

00:19:17.488 --> 00:19:20.292
Mike Shoemaker's
birthday tomorrow.

00:19:20.292 --> 00:19:21.527
And...

00:19:24.729 --> 00:19:29.467
It's a bummer because he makes
a huge deal out of it.

00:19:29.467 --> 00:19:32.671
I'm of the mind that people
of a certain age should stop

00:19:32.671 --> 00:19:34.740
making huge deals
out of their birthday.

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:38.810
Shoemaker is well past that age.

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:44.149
And yet every year, you know,
he rents out some weird room

00:19:44.149 --> 00:19:47.686
in the Bronx
that we all have to go to.

00:19:47.686 --> 00:19:50.956
It's usually in the basement
of some weird Catholic Church.

00:19:52.757 --> 00:19:54.893
[ Cell phone dings ]

00:19:57.262 --> 00:19:59.464
And the worst part is he does
the same thing every year,

00:19:59.464 --> 00:20:02.167
which is we all show up
and he's not there.

00:20:02.167 --> 00:20:06.538
And then they roll
in one of those big cakes,

00:20:06.538 --> 00:20:09.240
that, like,
a lady jumps out of.

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:14.245
And every year, it's him,
but he, like, makes us wait

00:20:14.245 --> 00:20:16.381
and we have to go through
this whole like weird

00:20:16.381 --> 00:20:17.849
like "Where is he?
I don't know where he is."

00:20:17.849 --> 00:20:22.720
And he's inside this cake
and like the cardboard.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:24.555
There's something about
the cardboard or the cake

00:20:24.555 --> 00:20:26.624
like it like makes
his allergies really bad.

00:20:26.624 --> 00:20:28.794
So he's just you hear him like
[imitates coughing]

00:20:31.563 --> 00:20:33.865
Like inside the cake,
just like hacking coughs.

00:20:33.865 --> 00:20:35.767
And we all have to be like,
"Oh, I wish he was here.

00:20:35.767 --> 00:20:39.804
He's not here on his birthday."

00:20:39.804 --> 00:20:41.339
And meanwhile, like,
he can't see us,

00:20:41.339 --> 00:20:44.676
we're all just like rolling her
eyes at each other.

00:20:44.676 --> 00:20:48.780
And then
and then he pops out like nine

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:51.182
we give you tells us to get
there at 6:30.

00:20:51.182 --> 00:20:55.119
It's like nine o'clock he,
like, pops out of the cake.

00:20:55.119 --> 00:20:59.157
And he's always like, "Who's
your [Bleep] O. Henry now?"

00:21:04.262 --> 00:21:05.397
See me next week.

